# Reflection loop

How this project improves its own way of working. Friction is written down the moment it
happens; every so often a **reflection pass** reads what is new, finds what keeps
recurring, and turns it into a small fix (a note, a nudge, a check). Small reversible fixes
are applied at once; anything that changes how the project is governed waits for
{{owner}}'s yes.

The loop has five steps: **capture → review → route → land → audit.** The procedure is in
the `reflect` skill; this doc is the reasoning and the record formats.

## 1. Capture: what gets written down, and by whom

| Record | Written by | One row means |
|---|---|---|
| `logs/failures.jsonl` | the `reflect-on-failure` hook | a research command failed |
| `logs/friction.jsonl` | the `friction` skill (agent or owner), hooks | something went badly or wasted effort |
| `logs/feedback.jsonl` | the agent, when the owner corrects or praises; gate bypasses | the owner reacted to how the work was done |
| `logs/nudges.jsonl` | `hooks/_payload.py`, automatically | a nudge fired (which hook, which key) |
| `notes/workflow-journal.md` | anyone, in prose; each reflection pass | a note too long for one log row, and the record of each pass |
| `logs/proposals.jsonl` | `bin/journal --propose` and `--decide` | a change waiting for {{owner}}'s decision, or the decision |

Each log is one JSON object per line; the writer adds `ts` and `id`. For the fields of a
friction, feedback or journal row, see `bin/journal --help` (the `--kind`, `--source`,
`--label` and `--now` options). Two formats are not covered there:

- **failures**: `{"signature": "<label>", "command": "<first line, ≤200 chars>", "excerpt": "<matching output line>"}`.
  The signature is the label of the `[[stack.failure_signatures]]` pattern that matched, or
  `<program>-exit-nonzero` when the command simply exited non-zero.
- **journal**: a Markdown bullet `- [YYYY-MM-DD] KIND: text`. `KIND` is `NOTE` by default,
  `REFLECT` for a pass's own record{{#if modules.retro}}, `RETRO` for a retrospective's counterfactual{{/if}}.

Write rows with `bin/journal --add` (it fills `ts` and `id` and checks the fields), never by hand in
an editor: a hand-edited JSONL line is easy to break and impossible to tell from a real row.

The capture rules:

- **Log your own friction in the same turn you notice it** — a failed launch, a step you
  had to redo, a wrong assumption, a convention that got in the way: `bin/journal --add
  friction "<one line>"`. *Why:* friction that waits for "later" is not logged, and a pass
  can only fix what it can see. *Anti-scope:* routine dead ends of research (a conjecture
  that turned out false) are results, not friction.
- **When the owner corrects how you work, log it as feedback** (`--kind correction`); a
  correction about the mathematics belongs in the research record, and when it corrects a
  *kind* of step (not a one-off fact) also log one line as `--kind math`, so a pass can
  propose a scan or a reviewer instruction when that kind recurs. *Why:* repeated
  corrections are the strongest signal the setup is missing a rule. *Anti-scope:* don't log
  ordinary instructions ("now do X") as corrections.
- **Failures are captured by the hook**, which acts only on the programs listed in
  `project.toml` `[stack] research_commands` and is silent until a signature recurs. With
  that list empty it does nothing. Add a `[[stack.failure_signatures]]` pattern when a tool
  reports failure with exit status 0 (a "stack overflow" line, a "did not converge"
  warning).
- **Every few turns with nothing logged, a reminder asks** whether anything went wrong
  (`[reflect] friction_nudge_every` in `project.toml`; 0 turns it off).

## 2. Review: what is "new", exactly

`bin/journal --show-new` prints every row not yet reviewed, from all four records at once,
and remembers exactly which rows it printed. `bin/journal --mark-reviewed` marks those rows
— and only those — as reviewed, and inserts a `<!-- reviewed YYYY-MM-DD -->` marker into the
journal where the printed entries ended.

- **Never buried:** a row logged *during* a pass was not printed, so it stays new for the
  next pass. That is why `--show-new` runs once, at the start, and is not re-run before
  `--mark-reviewed`.
- **Never double-counted:** rows are identified by their content, so a row already reviewed,
  or duplicated by a git merge, is not shown again.
- Review state lives in `logs/state/` (not committed): a fresh clone shows every row as new
  once. The journal marker *is* committed, so journal notes are not re-shown.

## 3. Route: which fixes are applied, which are asked

Draft at most four fixes, one per cluster that warrants one; a pass that finds nothing worth
changing says so. First place each cluster on the **ladder**:

1. **Prose** — a sentence in a discipline doc or skill. Right for a first occurrence or a
   judgment-heavy problem.
2. **Nudge** — a hook that refuses once with an explanation and lets an identical retry
   through. Right when the friction **recurred despite existing prose**: the recurrence is
   the evidence that prose is not holding, so do not propose the same sentence again.
3. **Hard check** — a hook or script that blocks. Only when a nudge is demonstrably ignored
   and the failure is costly; always with a stated bypass.

*Why the ladder:* a rule nobody reads at the right moment does not change behaviour, but a
check on every action costs everyone forever; climb one rung at a time, on evidence.
*Anti-scope:* keeping a re-violated rule as prose is allowed if you say why (the check would
fire falsely too often, or the case is irreducibly a judgment call).

Often the right fix is a **setting**, not a rule. The interview started this project on
defaults instead of asking the owner for details they could not know yet; those defaults are
tuned here, from what actually happened:

- a run that printed a failure but exited normally → a `[[stack.failure_signatures]]`
  pattern in `project.toml` (the defaults cover only the common cases of the project's tools);
- the owner correcting the same wording in write-ups twice → a rule in `[hygiene] rules`;
{{#if modules.experiments}}
- runs stopped by the watchdog that should have been allowed, or that ran far too long →
  `[experiments.limits]`;
{{/if}}
- a new tool the agents now run → `[stack] research_commands`.

Then route each fix by [the autonomy heuristics](../../notes/reflect-autonomy-heuristics.md),
which holds the full AUTO and ASK lists. An AUTO fix is applied in the pass; an ASK fix is
proposed and waits. A boundary case is ASK.

**Scope every proposal** with two parts: the *narrowest* class the evidence supports, and
an *anti-scope* (what it must not gate, its collateral cost). Then reread it as the owner
will — someone who knows the project but did not see this session — and spell out every
reference to this session's events or shorthand.

## 4. Land

- An AUTO fix is one commit with the subject `chore(reflect auto): <what>`, and one journal
  line `REFLECT: [auto-applied] <what> — <why> (rule: AUTO-n; undo: git revert <sha>)`. `AUTO-n` is the numbered AUTO clause in
  the heuristics file that justified applying it.
- A hook fix is self-tested on all four branches before it lands
  ([hook authoring](hook-authoring.md)).
- An ASK proposal is queued with `bin/journal --propose` (one row in `logs/proposals.jsonl`
  and a journal line) and put to the owner with its tradeoff, numbered among all pending
  proposals (`bin/journal --pending`); `bin/journal --shown` records that it was. The owner's
  answer is recorded with `bin/journal --decide <id> yes|no`, which also writes the journal
  line (`approved …` or `declined … — <reason>`, so it is not proposed again unchanged). An
  approved change is applied and committed in the same turn (subject `chore(reflect): <what>`).
- *Why a queue:* a proposal written only into a notes file, or mentioned once at the end of
  a long report, is never decided; the owner says "continue" and it is lost. Until it is
  decided, the `proposals-nudge` hook brings it back: a new proposal is listed at the end of
  the turn that queued it, and anything still pending gets a one-line reminder once per
  session. A "continue" decides nothing.
- Close the pass with `bin/journal --mark-reviewed`.

## 5. Audit: is AUTO routing still trusted?

`git log --oneline --grep='reflect auto'` lists every auto-applied fix. At each pass, note
which of them the owner reverted, narrowed or objected to. The agent running the pass records
each pass in the heuristics file's tables. If an auto-applied fix draws an objection of the "one incident made into a
rule" or "widened a permission" kind, the AUTO clause was too loose: tighten the clause,
don't just revert the fix.

## When to run a pass

At a natural stopping point, never in the middle of a task: when a failure nudge suggests
it, when several friction rows have piled up, or when {{owner}} asks. A pass is run by the
coordinating agent in the main session, not by a worker.
{{#if modules.retro}}

A **retrospective** (the `retro` skill; follow its steps) is the slower sibling: it reads
the owner's messages over recent sessions, labels each correction, and asks what structure
would have made the correction unnecessary.
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.program_review}}

A **program review** (the `program-review` skill) steps back further: it checks the
project's direction against [the program goals](../../notes/program-goals.md).
{{/if}}
